  "query_preview": "/MONDAY THROUGH FRIDAY/...\nIssued at 129 PM CST Sat Nov 14 2026\n\nEastern Iowa, northwest Illinois, and far northeast Missouri are \nexpected to remain under high pressure at the surface and aloft\nthrough Wednesday. Surface high pressure is expected to be just far\nenough to the east MOnday onward to turn light surface winds to\nthe south and allow for a gradual warming trend through the \nperiod. Temperatures warm from the lower 50s on Monday to the \nupper 50s on Wednesday. \n\nOn Thursday, flow aloft becomes southwesterly as a trough lifts \nnortheastward across the area. Despite what looks like better lift \nmoving into the area and southerly flow at the surface, moisture is \nlimited so any precipitation that does occur looks like it will be \nlight (less than a tenth of an inch) at this time. The southwest \nflow aloft remains in place through the remainder of the period with \nanother 500 mb trough lifting across the area Friday into Saturday. \nMoisture does look better but confidence on the timing is low and \nthe exact track of this storm system. High temperatures Thursday\nand Friday slowly warm into the lower 60s.",
